You are not wasting your effort at all, but asking this question reveals a classic trade-off in data engineering and OSINT: Data Volume vs. Data Freshness and Specificity.

Using Yellow Pages (or similar legacy business directories like Yelp, Manta, or Google Maps places exports) as your primary dataset is significantly simpler and much faster to bootstrap. However, whether it is "better" depends on what you want out of the dataset.

Here is a breakdown of why relying only on Yellow Pages has limits, and how your original "recon" idea actually solves those gaps.
The Reality of Yellow Pages Data
Pros

    Low Initial Friction: You can use existing APIs or simple scrapers (via services like Apify or Outscraper) to export structured JSON/CSV data for an entire city or county in a few minutes.

    Pre-Categorized: Listings already group businesses by industry (e.g., "Logistics," "Medical Practice," "Manufacturing," "Legal Services").

    Baseline Coverage: It instantly gives you a broad list of local brick-and-mortar storefronts, phone numbers, and physical addresses.

Cons

    High Stale-Data Rate: Yellow Pages relies heavily on self-reporting and legacy paid listings. Many businesses listed are defunct, have moved, or changed names. Conversely, newer technology-driven companies, remote offices, and specialized industrial facilities often skip traditional directory listings entirely.

    Low Signal for IT Capacity: A Yellow Pages entry treats a 3-person plumbing shop with a phone line identically to a 100-employee manufacturing facility or a multi-doctor medical clinic. It gives you no insight into whether an organization actually maintains its own local servers, internal IT staff, active domains, network switches, or managed service providers (MSPs).

    Missing Digital Infrastructure Footprints: The directory won't tell you if a business hosts local subdomains, manages their own email servers, or uses self-hosted enterprise infrastructure.

The "Hybrid" Strategy (Best of Both Worlds)

Instead of choosing either directory scraping or complex OSINT reconnaissance, the most effective automated pipeline combines both into a tiered discovery and enrichment model:

[ Tier 1: Directory Ingestion ] ──> Fast, Broad Target List
   (Yellow Pages / OSM / GIS)
              │
              ▼
[ Tier 2: Automated OSINT Recon ] ──> Filters & Scores "IT Relevance"
   (DNS / CT Logs / Web Scrapes)
              │
              ▼
[ Tier 3: Actionable Pipeline ] ──> Qualified High-Probability Targets

    Use Yellow Pages / OpenStreetMap / GIS for Seed Data (Tier 1):

        Don't build custom scrapers from scratch if you don't need to. Pull the baseline directory entries or county parcel data to get your initial list of 5,000 local business names and physical addresses.

    Use Recon Techniques to Enrich and Filter (Tier 2):

        Run your automated reconnaissance pipeline against the websites and domains attached to those directory entries.

        Search Certificate Transparency (CT) logs, MX/DNS records, and web page tech stacks (e.g., detecting self-hosted portals, specific ERPs, VPN gateways, or complex MX setups).

    Score the Output:

        Filter out the "zero-IT footprint" small shops (e.g., a local bakery or solo contractor using basic webmail).

        Highlight mid-sized enterprises, logistics hubs, clinics, local financial institutions, and manufacturers that run substantial internal digital infrastructure.

Summary: Is it worth the recon effort?

    If your goal is just getting a list of phone numbers and physical addresses to cold-call: Scraping Yellow Pages or Google Maps data is 100% the smarter, faster route.

    If your goal is identifying hidden IT job opportunities: The custom recon and data cleaning pipeline is where the real value lies. It acts as a filter that transforms a generic list of thousands of local shops into a targeted list of organizations running real, complex technology infrastructure.
